  "response": "```python\ndef bubble_sort(arr):\n  \"\"\"Sorts an array of strings using bubble sort.\n\n  Args:\n    arr: A list of strings to be sorted.\n\n  Returns:\n    The sorted list of strings.\n  \"\"\"\n  n = len(arr) \n  for i in range(n):\n    # Last i elements are already in place\n    for j in range(0, n-i-1):\n      # Swap if the element found is greater than the next element\n      if arr[j] > arr[j+1]:\n        arr[j], arr[j+1] = arr[j+1], arr[j]\n  return arr\n\n# Example usage:\na = [\"banana\", \"apple\", \"cherry\", \"date\", \"fig\"]  # Replace with your 500 strings\nsorted_a = bubble_sort(a)\nprint(f\"Sorted array: {sorted_a}\") \n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n1. **`bubble_sort(arr)` Function:**\n   - Takes an array `arr` of strings as input.\n   - Initializes `n` to the length of the array.\n   - Uses two nested loops:\n     - The outer loop (`for i in range(n)`) iterates through the array `n` times.\n     - The inner loop (`for j in range(0, n-i-1)`) compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. \n       -  The `n-i-1` limit is because the last `i` elements are already sorted after each pass of the outer loop.\n\n2. **Swapping:**\n   - The line `arr[j], arr[j+1] = arr[j+1], arr[j]` elegantly swaps the values of two array elements using simultaneous assignment.\n\n3. **Return:**\n   - The function returns the sorted array `arr`.\n\n4. **Example Usage:**\n   - Creates a sample array `a` (replace this with your 500 strings).\n   - Calls the `bubble_sort` function to sort the array and stores the result in `sorted_a`.\n   - Prints the sorted array.\n\n\n\n**Important Notes:**\n\n- **Efficiency:** Bubble sort is not very efficient for large arrays. Its time complexity is O(n^2) in the worst and average cases, meaning it becomes significantly slower as the size of the array increases. Consider using more efficient sorting algorithms like merge sort or quicksort for larger datasets.\n- **String Comparison:** Python handles string comparison naturally. When you use `>` or `<` with strings, it lexicographically compares them (letter by letter) to determine their order.\n\n\n\nLet me know if you have any other questions!",
